# Incentives Theory — Core



## The question

\textbf{Why don't workers exert first-best (socially efficient) effort?}

OR

\textbf{How can firms design incentives to increase worker productivity?}



## The key problem
$\bullet$ First layer: \textbf{Effort $e$ is unobservable} (hidden action / moral hazard), so contracts cannot be written directly on effort. \textit{Ross (1973), \emph{The Economic Theory of Agency: The Principal's Problem}; Holmström (1979), \emph{Moral Hazard and Observability}}

$\bullet$ Second layer: firms therefore compensate workers using \textbf{observable performance measures} (individual output $s_i$, team output, profits, supervisor evaluations, customer satisfaction, etc.), which are generally noisy and incomplete proxies for effort.
\hfill \textit{Holmström (1979), \emph{Moral Hazard and Observability}; Holmström \& Milgrom (1987), \emph{Aggregation and Linearity in the Provision of Intertemporal Incentives}}

$\rightarrow$ If a worker knows that greater effort will not be rewarded (flat pay), or will only be imperfectly rewarded because performance measures are noisy, incentives to exert effort are weakened.
\hfill \textit{Holmström \& Milgrom (1987), \emph{Aggregation and Linearity in the Provision of Intertemporal Incentives}}

\vspace{0.5em}

\footnotesize
$^1$ Much of the incentives literature can be interpreted as extensions of these two informational problems.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Multitasking}: firms observe only some dimensions of effort, so strong incentives distort effort toward measurable tasks.
\hfill \textit{Holmström \& Milgrom (1991), \emph{Multitask Principal--Agent Analyses: Incentive Contracts, Asset Ownership, and Job Design}}

\item \textbf{Career concerns}: future wages depend on the market's inference about worker ability from observed performance, creating dynamic incentives.
\hfill \textit{Holmström (1982), \emph{Managerial Incentive Problems: A Dynamic Perspective}}

\item \textbf{Team production / Free-riding}: when only aggregate output is observable, workers do not fully internalize the benefits of their effort.
\hfill \textit{Alchian \& Demsetz (1972), \emph{Production, Information Costs, and Economic Organization}; Holmström (1982), \emph{Moral Hazard in Teams}}

\item \textbf{Relative performance evaluation / Tournaments}: when absolute performance is noisy, rewarding workers according to rank filters out common shocks.
\hfill \textit{Lazear \& Rosen (1981), \emph{Rank-Order Tournaments as Optimum Labor Contracts}}

\item \textbf{Ratchet effect}: workers may deliberately underperform or hide productivity today to avoid tougher future performance targets.
\hfill \textit{Weitzman (1980), \emph{The ``Ratchet Principle'' and Performance Incentives}; Freixas, Guesnerie \& Tirole (1985), \emph{Planning under Incomplete Information and the Ratchet Effect}}
\end{itemize}

\normalsize

We therefore study two questions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Which observable performance measure best proxies hidden effort?}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Individual output (piece rates)
        \hfill \textit{Holmström \& Milgrom (1987), \emph{Aggregation and Linearity in the Provision of Intertemporal Incentives}; Lazear (2000), \emph{Performance Pay and Productivity}}

        \item Team output
        \hfill \textit{Holmström (1982), \emph{Moral Hazard in Teams}}

        \item Relative performance (rankings)
        \hfill \textit{Lazear \& Rosen (1981), \emph{Rank-Order Tournaments as Optimum Labor Contracts}}

        \item Supervisor evaluations / subjective performance
        \hfill \textit{Baker, Gibbons \& Murphy (1994), \emph{Subjective Performance Measures in Optimal Incentive Contracts}}

        \item Customer satisfaction and other subjective metrics
        \hfill \textit{Baker, Gibbons \& Murphy (1994), \emph{Subjective Performance Measures in Optimal Incentive Contracts}}
    \end{itemize}

\item \textbf{Which compensation instrument best aligns incentives?}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Flat wages (benchmark)

        \item Piece rates / bonuses
        \hfill \textit{Holmström \& Milgrom (1987), \emph{Aggregation and Linearity in the Provision of Intertemporal Incentives}; Lazear (2000), \emph{Performance Pay and Productivity}}

        \item Deferred compensation
        \hfill \textit{Lazear (1979), \emph{Why Is There Mandatory Retirement?}}

        \item Promotions
        \hfill \textit{Lazear \& Rosen (1981), \emph{Rank-Order Tournaments as Optimum Labor Contracts}}

        \item Compensation gaps within the hierarchy (pay dispersion)
        \hfill \textit{Rosen (1986), \emph{Prizes and Incentives in Elimination Tournaments}; Bloom (1999), \emph{The Performance Effects of Pay Dispersion on Individuals and Organizations}}

        \item Equity compensation / stock options
        \hfill \textit{Jensen \& Murphy (1990), \emph{Performance Pay and Top-Management Incentives}}

        \item Efficiency wages
        \hfill \textit{Shapiro \& Stiglitz (1984), \emph{Equilibrium Unemployment as a Worker Discipline Device}}
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
